00:17:41.960 --> 00:17:44.020
So how should you think
about moving forward

00:17:44.020 --> 00:17:45.235
with your migration journey?

00:17:47.790 --> 00:17:51.670
A good migration plan is
going to have four key phases.

00:17:51.670 --> 00:17:53.850
First, your
assessment phase where

00:17:53.850 --> 00:17:57.710
you work to understand
exactly what you have already.

00:17:57.710 --> 00:17:59.840
Secondly, based on
that assessment,

00:17:59.840 --> 00:18:01.820
you start to plan
what you should move

00:18:01.820 --> 00:18:04.490
and how you should move it.

00:18:04.490 --> 00:18:08.730
Third, you start to execute
the migration itself.

00:18:08.730 --> 00:18:13.110
And finally, you fine tune and
optimize what you've migrated.

00:18:13.110 --> 00:18:15.570
I think this should
sound pretty familiar.

00:18:15.570 --> 00:18:17.880
GG just talked about the
customer who actually

00:18:17.880 --> 00:18:21.210
went through these type
of four key phases,

00:18:21.210 --> 00:18:24.512
and they're actually still
going through that journey.

00:18:24.512 --> 00:18:26.720
And I realized that we're
representing this migration

00:18:26.720 --> 00:18:29.540
plan as a single circle,
but in reality, it's

00:18:29.540 --> 00:18:32.450
a bit more recursive
in that you'll

00:18:32.450 --> 00:18:34.880
have your high-level
migration plan,

00:18:34.880 --> 00:18:37.280
but then you also have
groups of applications called

00:18:37.280 --> 00:18:42.830
migration waves that have their
own specific plans as well,

00:18:42.830 --> 00:18:44.930
and each subsequent
plan in theory

00:18:44.930 --> 00:18:48.750
is adapting the lessons from
previous migration waves.

00:18:48.750 --> 00:18:51.420
In addition, it's not
uncommon to go back and forth

00:18:51.420 --> 00:18:54.270
between each of the
four different phases.

00:18:54.270 --> 00:18:57.450
It's all about building
that migration muscle memory

00:18:57.450 --> 00:18:59.190
and realizing that
this is a journey.

